## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in implementing observability across Kafka-based event streaming pipelines—a critical real-world challenge as data flows through multiple topics and partitions.

## Key Concepts
- W3C Trace Context propagation across messaging systems
- Span continuity across producer/consumer boundaries
- Sampling strategy selection and trace baggage for correlation
- Observability during partition rebalance and backpressure scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Inject trace context into outgoing message headers
function injectTraceContext(payload, tracer) {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('produce_order');
  const carrier = {};
  tracer.inject(span.context(), W3C_TRACE_CONTEXT, carrier);
  return { payload, headers: carrier };
}

// Extract trace context from incoming message
function extractTraceContext(message, tracer) {
  const spanContext = tracer.extract(W3C_TRACE_CONTEXT, message.headers);
  const span = tracer.startSpan('process_order', { childOf: spanContext });
  return span;
}

// Attach trace_id to enriched payload for cross-topic correlation
function enrichWithTraceId(payload, span) {
  return { ...payload, trace_id: span.spanContext().traceId };
}
```

## Key Considerations
- Use OpenTelemetry for vendor-agnostic instrumentation
- Ensure trace context propagation survives partition rebalances
- Consider tail-based sampling for cost-effective tracing of rare errors
- Monitor latency degradation during rebalance events

## Why This Question Matters
This question tests your ability to handle late data gracefully without breaking at-least-once delivery guarantees. It probes your understanding of per-topic state management, routing decisions under delayed data conditions, and offset tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Per-topic watermarks for independent time-window tracking
- Late data routing based on watermark thresholds
- Offset tracking and message deduplication for at-least-once semantics
- Canary testing to validate both in-order and late-data paths

## Code Example
```javascript
class WatermarkManager {
  constructor(maxLagMs = 60000) {
    this.maxLagMs = maxLagMs;
    this.topicWatermarks = new Map();
    this.offsetStore = new Map();
  }

  processMessage(topic, partition, offset, timestamp) {
    const key = `${topic}-${partition}`;
    const currentWatermark = this.topicWatermarks.get(key) || 0;

    if (timestamp > currentWatermark) {
      this.topicWatermarks.set(key, timestamp);
    }

    const watermark = this.topicWatermarks.get(key);
    const lag = watermark - timestamp;

    this.offsetStore.set(key, offset);

    if (lag > this.maxLagMs) {
      return { route: 'late_events', topic, partition, offset };
    }

    return { route: 'main', topic, partition, offset };
  }
}
```

This implementation maintains per-topic watermarks, routes late messages appropriately, tracks offsets, and supports at-least-once delivery through committed offset storage.
